  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 AM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nToday-Sunday... A surface ridge axis remains extended over central \nFlorida today, and light southerly flow continues on the western \nextent of the ridge. Showers and occasional storms across the \ncoastal waters early this morning may brush portions of the coast,\nbut most areas should remain dry ahead of sunrise. Into late \nmorning and the early afternoon, development of the east coast sea\nbreeze should initiate a diurnal pattern of scattered showers and\nstorms. Coverage increases inland through the remainder of the \nafternoon and evening as the sea breeze interacts with prior storm\nboundaries and eventually collides with the west coast sea breeze\nnear the Orlando metro. On Sunday, the influence of the surface \nridge weakens, and southerly winds will become more light and \nvariable. Otherwise, a very similar pattern of diurnal showers and\nstorms is expected. Deep moisture will persist through the \nperiod, and heavy downpours within slow moving storms may result \nin localized rainfall accumulations of 2-3 inches. A few stronger\nstorms which develop will be capable of frequent lightning \nstrikes and localized downburst wind gusts of 40-50 mph each day.\n\nTemperatures remain seasonably hot, mostly reaching the upper 80s \nand lower 90s. Muggy conditions will allow peak heat index values to \nclimb into the triple digits, and values ranging 100-106F will \nremain below Heat Advisory criteria. Mild mornings continue with \nlows in the mid to upper 70s. \n\nMonday-Friday... Ridging aloft amplifies over the southeast U.S. \nearly next week as a trough digs through the northeast U.S. and \nmoves offshore. Surface high pressure quickly fills over the \nnortheastern seaboard behind the trough, increasing the pressure \ngradient. Local onshore winds increase in response, becoming breezy \nand gusty on Tuesday and further increasing to around 15-20 mph \non Wednesday. Beyond mid week, greater uncertainty exists within \nthe synoptic pattern. As the aforementioned trough continues to \nmove offshore, a broadening area of low pressure rounds the \neastern side of the mid-level ridge anchored over the southeast \nU.S. More aggressive deterministic model solutions show this area \nbecoming a closed low while most ensemble guidance shows a more \nbroad open wave developing over the southwest Atlantic by mid \nweek. The area of low pressure continues to round the decaying \nridge over the southeast U.S. into late week, passing over Florida\nand into the Gulf. Deep moisture looks to generally persists \nthrough the extended forecast, continuing a period of near to \nabove seasonal rain chances. Slightly above normal temperatures \nare forecast Monday as highs reach the low to mid 90s. Increasing \nonshore flow should then gradually reduce temperatures, with highs\nmostly ranging the upper 80s by Wednesday.",
